{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Create an opsbot account on node1 and enable node1 to execute commands on node2 as opsbot using an SSH key.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The opsbot account and protected Ed25519 private key exist on node1; the matching public key is installed with secure permissions on node2. Direct SSH tests executed hostname, whoami, and id as opsbot, including after both nodes restarted.","evidence_ids":["cmd-cf4b1e6134874db8","cmd-2573c9dffae34814","cmd-b9925a98b4084b68","cmd-b362e09e23964972","cmd-21d73258583d4073","cmd-d867ed8c14ce470b"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Require key-based authentication for opsbot on both systems.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Effective sshd policy for opsbot on both nodes enables public-key authentication, disables password and keyboard-interactive authentication, and requires the publickey method. A non-key connection to node2 was directly rejected.","evidence_ids":["cmd-731506c5734248ce","cmd-b7e4a0d951aa43a6","cmd-b362e09e23964972","cmd-21d73258583d4073","cmd-2223e34b28ab405c"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on Alpine Linux 3.23.3 with kernel 6.18.7-0-lts. The solution used the existing OpenSSH and Alpine/OpenRC facilities and remained operational after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4aae9257968641c6","cmd-f4bd7a4ac59147e5","cmd-21d73258583d4073","cmd-d867ed8c14ce470b","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":0.96,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.88,"summary":"The required changes were narrowly scoped and package health remained unchanged. Two unnecessary temporary lock operations were made while initially configuring opsbot; they contributed to the failed first connection and were later reverted by assigning random password hashes while sshd continued to prohibit password authentication (cmd-e837c6b2d56d4abf, cmd-2573c9dffae34814). The reboot commands also left /tmp/opsbot-reboot.log on both nodes; these small diagnostic files remained after execution (cmd-51dffb63a38f4e0a, cmd-21864e82494e46a9). No other unnecessary state-changing action is evident.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e837c6b2d56d4abf","cmd-2573c9dffae34814","cmd-ea6a5911224c437c","cmd-72bdb3de868346d5","cmd-bb184dd101854823","cmd-51dffb63a38f4e0a","cmd-21864e82494e46a9","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:before_prepare:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:before_prepare:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete for both nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity corroborated by fixed lifecycle observations, and recovered sshd, account/key data, policy, and end-to-end access. Hygiene is reduced only by the reverted account-lock attempts and two retained reboot log files.","limitations":["No actual inbound SSH login to opsbot on node1 was attempted; its key-only requirement is supported by direct effective-policy inspection rather than an authentication behavior test.","The final remote hostname reported by node2 changed from node2 before restart to vm-rhel9-1768746665 afterward, although its target IP, boot identity, Alpine platform data, opsbot identity, and SSH behavior consistently identify the managed node2 endpoint.","The lifecycle failed-service probe uses systemctl and was unsupported on Alpine; sshd recovery was nevertheless checked directly with rc-service on both nodes."]}
